# Every text file keeps LF in the working tree, on every platform. Git decides
# what is text; anything it detects as binary is left alone.
#
# The repository's toolchain is POSIX end to end — bash gates, a Makefile, and
# greps that are frequently anchored — and a Windows clone with the default
# `core.autocrlf=true` breaks all three at once. test/object_abi.sh is the
# clearest case: it asserts the runtime's ABI constants with
# `grep -q '^#define RT_HEADER_SIZE 16$' runtime/beans_rt.c`, and `$` anchors
# before the newline but *after* a carriage return, so on a Windows checkout
# the check that guards the object ABI silently could not match.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out below as well, because each of these was found the hard way and
# the reason is worth keeping next to the rule.

# Shell scripts must keep LF in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# This is not a style preference. Git stores them as LF, but a Windows clone
# with the default `core.autocrlf=true` checks them out as CRLF, and bash then
# reads `set -euo pipefail\r` and reports "pipefail: invalid option name". Every
# gate in test/ dies on its second line. That includes the two gates whose whole
# purpose is to run *on Windows* — test/windows_hosted.sh under Git Bash and
# test/windows_native_run.sh on the windows runner — so without this the
# Windows story is untestable on a stock Windows checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf

# make treats a trailing CR as part of the command it runs, so a recipe becomes
# `clang++ ...\r` and the shell reports a command it appears to spell correctly.
Makefile text eol=lf
*.mk text eol=lf

# The manifests the test scripts parse field-by-field. A trailing CR silently
# becomes part of the last field, which turns an exit-code column into a string
# that never compares equal.
*.tsv text eol=lf

# Documentation checks use line-oriented shell tools. Pin Markdown to LF so
# those checks behave the same way on Windows, Linux, and macOS checkouts.
*.md text eol=lf

